    <h1>Who Gets to Discover an Auteur?</h1>
    <p class="essay-deck">The grammar of praise around Joanna Hogg</p>
    <p class="essay-byline">Hanpu Li</p>
    <p class="essay-note">This essay was rebuilt for the web from a 2026 research project. The argument and source attributions have been narrowed after a fresh audit.</p>
  </header>

  <div class="essay-body">
    <p>Peter Bradshaw began his 2008 review of <em>Unrelated</em> by announcing that Joanna Hogg had appeared “as if from nowhere”. He ended by asking what a “find” British cinema had in her. Between those two moments, Hogg becomes a discovered object: already complete, waiting for the critic to recognise her.</p>

    <p>The praise is real. Bradshaw describes the fixed camera, the distant sound and the poolside shot in which the film refuses to cut towards the row taking place indoors. He is responding to choices that are there on screen. Yet the review does more than describe those choices. It gathers them under Hogg’s name, turns them into proof of a coherent author and presents that author as something criticism has found.</p>

    <p>The small grammatical moves are more revealing than another vote on whether Hogg is an auteur. Who acts in the sentence? Which comparison supplies prestige? When a film has many credited makers, whose name is allowed to stand for its look? A claim about an artist’s essence can drift indefinitely. A verb, a possessive and a comparison are on the page.</p>

    <section aria-labelledby="hogg-history-heading">
      <h2 id="hogg-history-heading">1. A discovery with a history</h2>

      <p>The broad argument was not new when I began this research. In 2015, Ciara Barrett showed that criticism had repeatedly explained Hogg through an “overwhelmingly male canon”, including Ozu, Rohmer, Haneke, Bresson and Antonioni. Barrett also identified the cost of that praise. Critics defended Hogg’s originality by locating her style inside an existing lineage, then treated that lineage as the source from which her work became legible.</p>

      <p>Any account of Hogg and the male auteur canon has to start there. The six texts examined here allow a change of scale. Barrett established the pattern across Hogg’s early reception; these pages show how particular verbs assign action to Hogg, how a comparison directs value and how collaboration disappears when authorship becomes singular.</p>

      <p>That change of scale matters because it prevents an attractive argument from becoming larger than its evidence. I examined six texts attached to <em>Unrelated</em> (2007), <em>Exhibition</em> (2013) and <em>The Souvenir</em> (2019): three Guardian reviews by Bradshaw and three texts collected in BFI Southbank programme notes. This is a deliberately small corpus. Half of it comes from one critic, and all of it is admiring. It can show how these texts work. It cannot establish how British film criticism works as a whole.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="hogg-corpus-heading">
      <h2 id="hogg-corpus-heading">2. What the corpus can actually tell us</h2>

      <p>The source audit produced an immediate correction. My earlier project treated the programme-note texts as if they were written in an institutional BFI voice. On the BFI pages, the relevant <em>Unrelated</em> passage is credited to Catherine Wheatley and was first published in <em>Sight and Sound</em> in March 2008. The corresponding <em>Exhibition</em> and <em>Souvenir</em> passages are credited to Andrew Tracy and Kate Stables. The BFI selected and republished this criticism for a 2023 season, giving it a new institutional setting without becoming the author of the sentences.</p>

      <p>That correction weakens one part of my old argument. I had contrasted an institutional mode of attribution with Bradshaw’s critical one. In fact, I was comparing different critics presented through different outlets and later gathered by an institution. The BFI still matters as a frame, especially because the programme pages place criticism beside full production credits. It cannot be treated as the author of every sentence inside that frame.</p>

      <p>Another result becomes more modest under the same pressure. Bradshaw uses “middle-distance” to describe Hogg’s sound in his reviews of both <em>Unrelated</em> and <em>Exhibition</em>, six years apart. The repetition establishes a stable term in Bradshaw’s vocabulary, not an institutional consensus. A larger corpus would be needed to trace whether the phrase travelled among critics, press materials and programme notes.</p>

      <p>A small corpus makes individual operations visible. It does not convert recurrence into a law.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="hogg-directions-heading">
      <h2 id="hogg-directions-heading">3. One sentence, two directions</h2>

      <p>Wheatley’s <em>Unrelated</em> paragraph contains the most compressed example. Hogg is said to turn away from the “working-class realism” of Lynne Ramsay and Amma Asante. Her film is then placed beside the “pared-down narratives and painterly compositions” of Ozon and Rohmer.</p>

      <p>The sentence sends its comparisons in two directions. Ramsay and Asante name the British tradition that Hogg is leaving. Ozon and Rohmer name the art-cinema company she is entering. The women mark distance; the men supply resemblance and affiliation. Prestige reaches Hogg through the second comparison.</p>

      <p>The asymmetry is striking, and its boundary has to remain visible. One paragraph cannot support a general rule that female directors always rank while male directors always provide lineage. Asante’s position as a Black British filmmaker also gives the comparison a racial dimension that a gender-only account cannot explain. The evidence supports this close reading and a question for a larger study, not a settled law of reception.</p>

      <p>The sentence is still worth dwelling on because nothing in it is overtly dismissive. Ramsay and Asante are established filmmakers. Ozon and Rohmer are not introduced as masters whose approval Hogg needs. The hierarchy appears through direction and function: which names mark departure, and which names make the destination intelligible.</p>

      <p>The grammar of praise matters because a generous-looking list of names can still assign each name a different job.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="hogg-collaborator-heading">
      <h2 id="hogg-collaborator-heading">4. The interchangeable cinematographer</h2>

      <p>Bradshaw’s review of <em>The Souvenir</em> makes the allocation of authorship unusually clear. He describes a look Hogg has created across her films “no matter what cinematographer she is working with”. The cinematographer enters the sentence only to become interchangeable. Hogg remains the continuous source of the image.</p>

      <p>The programme notes make the missing labour easy to name. They credit Oliver Curtis as director of photography on <em>Unrelated</em>, Ed Rutherford on <em>Exhibition</em> and David Raedeker on <em>The Souvenir</em>. They also list editors, production designers, sound workers and camera crews. The credits do not tell us how every decision was made, and they do not prove that the recurring visual choices belong less to Hogg. They show that the films were made through changing collaborations.</p>

      <p>Bradshaw’s sentence solves that complexity in advance. Difference among cinematographers becomes evidence for the director: if the look persists while the collaborator changes, the look must originate with Hogg. The collaborator is acknowledged, then used to strengthen singular authorship.</p>

      <p>There is a reasonable defence of this move. A director can pursue related formal choices with different crews, and a critic needs some way to discuss continuity across a body of work. The problem is not the existence of directorial continuity. It is the sentence’s confidence about where continuity comes from. The critic does not investigate how Hogg, Raedeker, Rutherford, Curtis and their departments produced those images. Grammar has already settled the credit.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="hogg-discovery-heading">
      <h2 id="hogg-discovery-heading">5. Praise as a procedure</h2>

      <p>The same review says that Hogg “has found a contemporary English response” to Antonioni and Visconti. The phrase praises her intelligence and specificity, but it also gives the older male directors priority. Their cinema is the proposition. Hogg supplies the response.</p>

      <p>This resembles the opening of the <em>Unrelated</em> review. Hogg arrives from nowhere, while the critical language around her arrives from somewhere very recognisable. Fixed framing, long takes, spare close-ups, difficult tone and middle-distance sound become legible through an established art-cinema vocabulary. Comparison makes the new filmmaker easier to place. The critic then experiences that placement as discovery.</p>

      <p>I do not think this makes the praise false. Hogg’s films do use duration, off-screen action, architectural framing and withheld reaction in ways that deserve close attention. Nor does every comparison with Antonioni or Rohmer reduce her work. Comparisons can identify formal relations that would otherwise remain vague. The question is what the comparison does after the resemblance has been named. Does it open a conversation between films, or does it turn the older director into the measure by which the newer one acquires seriousness?</p>

      <p>That distinction cannot be decided from the comparator’s gender alone. It depends on the verb, the direction of explanation and what the sentence allows the newer filmmaker to originate. In this corpus, the most revealing phrases are ordinary ones: Hogg appears, finds, eschews, crafts and contrives. Each verb carries a small theory of authorship.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="hogg-audit-heading">
      <h2 id="hogg-audit-heading">6. What survives the audit</h2>

      <p>The strongest version of my earlier argument claimed that gendered anchoring was structurally necessary to auteur criticism. Six admiring texts cannot establish that, and one ambiguous example cannot show that reflexive criticism is unable to escape the structure. Calling the uncertainty itself a finding only protected the thesis from being tested.</p>

      <p>A narrower argument survives. Barrett had already shown that Hogg’s reception repeatedly routes her through a predominantly male canon. The texts examined here add several language-level mechanisms. A comparison can give female and male directors different jobs inside one sentence. A sentence can name a cinematographer in order to exclude cinematography from the authorship it constructs. Repeated vocabulary can make one critic’s established way of seeing feel like a new encounter with each film.</p>

      <p>These mechanisms do not expose a secret intention. They show a critical habit operating inside serious, attentive praise. The next time a director seems to arrive from nowhere, the route deserves as much attention as the arrival: who was removed from it, whose name supplied the destination, and which collaborators disappeared when the work became a signature?</p>
    </section>

